-- title:
--   Eq. (6) — conditional-mean representation of the score of a sum, $\rho_{N_1+N_2}(N_1+N_2)=\mathbb E[\rho_{N_1}(N_1)\mid N_1+N_2]$
-- statement:
--   Let $N_1$ and $N_2$ be independent real random variables on a probability space $(\Omega,P)$. Assume that $N_1$ has an absolutely continuous density $f_1$ with score $\rho_{N_1}=f_1'/f_1$ such that $\mathbb E|\rho_{N_1}(N_1)|<\infty$. Then $N_1+N_2$ has an absolutely continuous density, and its score satisfies, almost surely,
--   $$\rho_{N_1+N_2}(N_1+N_2)=\mathbb E\big[\rho_{N_1}(N_1)\,\big|\,N_1+N_2\big].$$
--   By symmetry the same holds with $N_2$ in place of $N_1$ (the case $i=2$ of eq. (6)).
--
--   This is the classical Stam–Blachman representation of the score of a sum. The paper applies it in Appendix A, eq. (31), with $N_1$ the Gaussian noise $W$, to compute the score of $N+W$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The conditional expectation $\mathbb E[\,\cdot\mid N_1+N_2=n]$ evaluated at $n=N_1+N_2$ is the conditional expectation given $\sigma(N_1+N_2)$, and the identity is an almost-sure one. Finite Fisher information $J(N_1)<\infty$ implies the hypotheses.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: i = 1, 2 → the statement for i = 1, for every independent pair; reason: the case i = 2 is the statement applied to (N₂, N₁), since independence is symmetric and N₂ + N₁ = N₁ + N₂
--   - encoding: the score function ρ_{N_i} and the conditional mean of ρ_{N_i}(N_i), presupposed to exist → hypotheses: the law of N₁ has an absolutely continuous density, and ρ_{N₁}(N₁) is integrable; reason: these are exactly the conditions under which the right side of (6) is defined; they exclude only undefined cases
--   - encoding: ρ_{N₁+N₂}, presupposed to be a score → the conclusion also asserts that N₁ + N₂ has an absolutely continuous density; reason: makes the left side the score of an actual density rather than the placeholder value of `score`; it is implicit in the page's use of ρ_{N₁+N₂}
-- source:
--   Liu–Viswanath, Two Proofs of the Fisher Information Inequality via Data Processing Arguments, arXiv:cs/0604028v1, eq. (6), p.2; used in eq. (31), p.5

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FisherInfoIneq_IsACDensity
import Definitions.Def_FisherInfoIneq_score

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace FisherInfoIneq

/-- **Display (6): conditional-mean representation of the score of a sum.** Let `N₁, N₂` be
independent real random variables on a probability space `(Ω, P)`, where `N₁` has an absolutely
continuous density whose score `ρ_{N₁} = f′/f` satisfies `E|ρ_{N₁}(N₁)| < ∞`. Then `N₁ + N₂` has an
absolutely continuous density and, `P`-almost surely,
`ρ_{N₁+N₂}(N₁ + N₂) = E[ρ_{N₁}(N₁) | N₁ + N₂]`.

Liu–Viswanath, *Two Proofs of the Fisher Information Inequality via Data Processing Arguments*,
arXiv:cs/0604028v1, eq. (6), p.2: "ρ_{N₁+N₂}(n) = E[ρ_{N_i}(N_i) | N₁ + N₂ = n], i = 1, 2";
used in Appendix A, eq. (31), p.5, with `N_i` the Gaussian `W`.

**Formalization Note**
1. The case `i = 2` is this statement applied to `(N₂, N₁)`: independence is symmetric and
   `N₂ + N₁ = N₁ + N₂`.
2. The hypotheses say exactly that the right side of (6) is defined: the score `ρ_{N₁}` exists
   (an absolutely continuous density) and `ρ_{N₁}(N₁)` is integrable, so its conditional mean is
   the classical one. Finite Fisher information `J(N₁) < ∞` implies them.
3. The first conclusion says that the score `ρ_{N₁+N₂}` on the left is the score of an actual
   absolutely continuous density of `N₁ + N₂`, not the placeholder value of `score`.
4. `E[· | N₁ + N₂ = n]` evaluated at `n = N₁ + N₂` is the conditional expectation given the
   σ-algebra generated by `N₁ + N₂`; the identity is an almost-sure one. -/
theorem score_sum_condExp {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (N₁ N₂ : Ω → ℝ) (hN₁ : Measurable N₁) (hN₂ : Measurable N₂) (hind : IndepFun N₁ N₂ P) (hAC : ∃ f g : ℝ → ℝ, IsACDensity (P.map N₁) f g) (hρ : Integrable (fun ω => score (P.map N₁) (N₁ ω)) P) : (∃ f g : ℝ → ℝ, IsACDensity (P.map (N₁ + N₂)) f g) ∧ (fun ω => score (P.map (N₁ + N₂)) (N₁ ω + N₂ ω)) =ᵐ[P] P[fun ω => score (P.map N₁) (N₁ ω) | MeasurableSpace.comap (N₁ + N₂) inferInstance] := by sorry

end FisherInfoIneq
